---
term: AI executive
section: execution
order: 40
definition: An artificial intelligence (AI) system that a corporation appoints as its [[executive agent]] to pursue the charter's [[objective function]] within the charter's constraints.
example: A regional water corporation appoints an AI executive to schedule reservoir releases. The charter sets the objective, which is supply reliability subject to a minimum river flow. The AI decides the release schedule each day.
seeAlso:
  - hybrid-executive
  - delegation-of-functions
---
An AI executive is an agent of the charter, not a source of political legitimacy. It can hold deep, high-autonomy authority over methods, but it can't redefine the interest it serves. Members keep [[constitutional authority]], and an independent [[measurement mechanism]] judges the executive's performance.

AI isn't the premise of the Social Charter. It's an especially capable executive and analytic technology that runs on top of the digital infrastructure that large-scale chartering needs.
